{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Remove the foreign gods.</b> These are the idols of Shechem, of silver and gold, which they took in the war that he mentioned. And even though all of it was permitted for use — since they had already annulled it, and a gentile annuls idolatry involuntarily, as is stated in Avodah Zarah — nevertheless he commanded to remove it from among them, and once annulled it was permitted to them. And the reason he commanded to remove it from among them was for the purity of holiness, so that they would be worthy to serve YHWH, may He be exalted, in purity, and to offer a sacrifice before Him, as he said, ״and let us arise and go up to Bethel,״ in the manner of what is written (Ecclesiastes 4): ״שמור רגלך כאשר תלך אל בית האלהים״." "<b>And purify yourselves.</b> Scripture teaches you that sin is called impurity (טומאה). And it is written (Ezekiel 20): ״ובגלולי מצרים אל תטמאו״, and whoever abandons sin is called pure, as it is stated (Leviticus 16): ״מכל חטאתיכם לפני ה׳ תטהרו״. And it is written (Joshua 22): ״המעט לנו את עון פעור אשר לא הטהרנו ממנו״, and David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 51): ״תחטאני באזוב ואטהר״, and it is written (Psalms 51): ״ומחטאתי טהרני״." "<b>And change your garments.</b> Scripture teaches you that they and their garments had become impure, whether from the impurity of idolatry or from the impurity of the slain of Shechem."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:17:42.509392387Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}